  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 232 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nMoisture transport will only ramp up Wednesday night into Thursday \nas a disturbance rotating around the west coast trough sweeps \nthrough the Four Corners region. At this point, soils across much of \nwestern and central NM will be saturated and it will take less \nrainfall than normal to produce flash flooding. Given the large \nscale forcing and moisture transport, showers and storms will \ncontinue into the overnight and even into the morning hours. This is \nexpected to limit afternoon destabilization and therefore rainfall \nrates, but the overall coverage of showers and storms will remain \nhigh regardless of potential rates. Temperatures will remain \nseasonably cool, with even chilly temperatures in the mountains \nwhere temps will struggle to reach 70 in the afternoon and should \ndrop into the 40s at night. \n\nThe west coast trough funneling moisture into the desert southwest \nwill begin to lift northwestward into the northern Rockies Friday, \ntilting the moisture plume to become more zonal and cutting off the \nsubtropical tap. However, there will still be plenty of lingering \nmoisture for showers and storms through Friday, with a more stark \ndrier trend on Saturday. The downtick will be short-lived since the \nsame trough exiting into the High Plains could send a backdoor front \ninto eastern NM that replenishes moisture once again.\n\nThe subtropical jet stream is forecast to become more active next \nweek, favoring the continuation of wet weather over the desert \nsouthwest. While technically still part of Monsoon Season, this \ntransitional pattern is much more Fall-like in nature with more \nactive westerlies.",
